elp, because the bound functions read the facade's globals.\n3. P2 — check_agent_spawn.py:600-602 says the REASON_* constants are \"at check_agent_spawn.py:188\"; they are now in _lib/agent_spawn/prompt_rules.py:93-106. check_agent_spawn.py:633 still calls the file \"this 1700-LoC file\". Fix the two references.\n4. P2 — check_agent_spawn.py:22-23, 50 and 642. These comments exist so .claude/scripts/gen-command-skill-hook-map.py, which derives guard involvement from literal mentions of `.claude/skills`/`SKILL.md` in registered hook source, keeps check_agent_spawn on the skill surface now that skill_reference.py lives under _lib/. The claim is fair (the bound helpers do validate SKILL.md references, and docs/COMMAND-SKILL-HOOK-MAP.md says its rule over-approximates). But the map row now rests on a comment, and deleting it drops the row silently. Fix: let the scanner follow a facade's _lib/<pkg> parts, or document the anchor.\n5. P2 (existed before this delta, not a regression) — kernel _lib/audit_emit.py:38 imports _lib/spool_writer.py, which is not kernel, and spool_writer.py:588 now hands off to _lib/_spool_writer_parts/, also not kernel. Both stay at the canonical/sentinel tier (_lib/**/*.py in canonical_edit/guards.py:34), exactly like spool_writer.py in v1.4.2. test_kernel_decomposition_coverage only follows imports out of kernel sources, so it can't see this hop. Follow-up: decide on purpose whether the audit spool writer belongs in _KERNEL_PATHS.","decision":"GO","sentence":"Both declared conditions this payload touches (5 and 7) hold against the code, the check_agent_spawn split moves the code without changing behavior or fail direction, and nothing at P0 or P1 was found, only P2 follow-ups."},"ttft_ms":5043,"type":"result","duration_ms":574406,"uuid":"aa8f27e8-d490-43c5-a8b5-b09140d9a960","ttft_stream_ms":3620,"time_to_request_ms":250,"first_content_frame_ms":3620,"queued_turn_count":0,"result_index":0,"model_usage_since_last_result":{"claude-opus-5-5":{"inp